 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 27,
   "y": 88,
   "text": "ANOTHER PLACE. A hundred cast-iron men stand along about three kilometres of Crosby beach, every one cast from the artist's own body, all gazing out to sea. They came here in 2005 for a few months and the town liked them so much they stayed for good in 2007. The tide covers some of them twice a day, and the barnacles have given several a beard.",
   "plaque": "lancashire_another_place",
   "theme": "arts",
   "title": "Another Place"
  },
  {
   "x": 31,
   "y": 80,
   "text": "CROSBY AND THE MERSEY. From this beach you watch the big ships slide into the Mersey on the tide, and on a clear day you can see the hills of North Wales and the wind turbines out at sea. The sand here is fine and the mud is finer: keep to the firm stuff.",
   "plaque": "lancashire_crosby_mersey",
   "title": "The Mouth of the Mersey"
  },
  {
   "x": 36,
   "y": 101,
   "text": "SOUTH: Liverpool, along the Dock Road. NORTH: Formby's pinewoods and Southport. Much further north: Blackpool, and the lights."
  },
  {
   "x": 27,
   "y": 50,
   "text": "FORMBY'S PINEWOODS. The pines were planted from the late 1800s to hold the shifting dunes still, and they became one of the last strongholds of the red squirrel in England. In 2008 squirrelpox, carried by grey squirrels, killed most of them. Rangers and volunteers kept the greys out, and the reds have been coming back ever since.",
   "plaque": "lancashire_formby_pines",
   "title": "Formby's Pinewoods"
  },
  {
   "x": 13,
   "y": 55,
   "text": "FOOTPRINTS IN THE MUD. When the sea scours the beach at Formby Point it uncovers layers of ancient mud printed with footprints thousands of years old: people, children, red deer, aurochs and wading birds, walking the shore of a world before farming. Each set is washed away again within days, so the beach keeps a diary nobody can keep.",
   "plaque": "lancashire_formby_footprints",
   "title": "Prehistoric Footprints"
  },
  {
   "x": 46,
   "y": 63,
   "text": "FORMBY ASPARAGUS. The light, sandy soil here once grew asparagus by the cartload, sent off on the railway to the dining cars and grand hotels. Most of the fields have gone back to dune and pine, but a few local growers still cut the spears each spring, in a season of only about eight weeks.",
   "plaque": "lancashire_formby_asparagus",
   "title": "Formby Asparagus"
  },
  {
   "x": 25,
   "y": 70,
   "text": "THE DUNE SLACKS. The hollows between the dunes flood in winter and dry out in summer: just right for natterjack toads, which need warm, shallow pools with no fish in them. On a spring night the males all call at once, a rattling chorus you can hear a kilometre away. They run, rather than hop, on short little legs.",
   "plaque": "lancashire_natterjack_slacks",
   "title": "The Dune Slacks"
  },
  {
   "x": 29,
   "y": 77,
   "text": "FORMBY. Red squirrels, please do not feed them crisps. National Trust rangers this way."
  },
  {
   "x": 15,
   "y": 13,
   "text": "SOUTHPORT PIER. Opened in 1860, it is one of the oldest iron piers in Britain and, at over a kilometre, the second longest after Southend's. The joke is that the sea at Southport is shy: at low tide it goes out so far that the end of the pier stands over sand, and visitors have been known to ask where the sea has gone.",
   "plaque": "lancashire_southport_pier",
   "title": "Southport Pier"
  },
  {
   "x": 25,
   "y": 6,
   "text": "LORD STREET. Southport's grand boulevard, a mile of shops behind iron-and-glass verandas, with gardens and trees down the middle. Local legend says the future Emperor Napoleon III lodged near here in the 1840s and took the idea home for the boulevards of Paris. Historians shrug. Southport does not.",
   "plaque": "lancashire_lord_street",
   "title": "Lord Street"
  },
  {
   "x": 29,
   "y": 24,
   "text": "SOUTHPORT FLOWER SHOW. Every August since 1924, apart from the war years, Victoria Park fills with marquees for one of the biggest independent flower shows in Britain. Next door, Royal Birkdale's links have hosted golf's Open Championship many times.",
   "plaque": "lancashire_southport_flowers",
   "title": "Southport Flower Show"
  },
  {
   "x": 37,
   "y": 20,
   "text": "MARTIN MERE. This was once one of the largest lakes in England, until it was drained for farmland from the 1690s. Since 1975 it has been a wetland reserve again, and each autumn thousands of whooper swans fly in from Iceland, with tens of thousands of pink-footed geese, to spend the winter here. The swans are fed in front of the hide on winter afternoons, and they know the time.",
   "plaque": "lancashire_martin_mere",
   "title": "Martin Mere"
  }
